1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,200
Lotte World Tower is 555 m tall. How long does the elevator take to the top?

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:09,300
About 1 minute. The sky deck elevator goes up at 10 m per second.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:14,900
It goes 496 m in one ride, from B2 to the 121st floor.

4
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:19,350
It is a double-deck elevator, so it carries people on two floors at once.

5
00:00:19,350 --> 00:00:25,150
The glass floor on the 118th floor is 478 m high. Each square meter holds 1 ton.

6
00:00:25,150 --> 00:00:29,400
There are 61 elevators. In a fire, 19 of them help people escape.

7
00:00:29,400 --> 00:00:34,850
108 concrete piles in rock, 38 m underground, hold all this weight.

8
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:39,850
While we reach the sky in 1 minute, giant roots hold on under the ground.

9
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:44,533
On the way to Incheon Airport, a bridge goes on and on over the sea.

10
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:47,533
It's Incheon Bridge, over 21 km long.

11
00:00:47,533 --> 00:00:52,533
In the middle are two towers, 238 m tall, as tall as the 63 Building.

12
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:56,483
Cables from the towers hold up the road like a fan.

13
00:00:56,483 --> 00:01:00,383
Thanks to that, the gap between pillars can be 800 m wide.

14
00:01:00,383 --> 00:01:04,283
Under it, giant container ships go in and out of Incheon Port.

15
00:01:04,283 --> 00:01:08,383
The mud under the sea is deep, so the pillars go all the way down to rock.

16
00:01:08,383 --> 00:01:12,733
In 2009, a 21 km road over the sea opened.

17
00:01:13,733 --> 00:01:17,478
Yeouido is full of tall buildings. Why is it so flat and straight?

18
00:01:17,478 --> 00:01:22,375
Long ago, it was a sand island in the Han River where the royal family kept sheep and goats.

19
00:01:22,375 --> 00:01:26,696
In 1916, an airport was built on this flat sand.

20
00:01:26,696 --> 00:01:30,440
But every rainy season, the whole island went under water.

21
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:35,626
In 1968, a 7.6 km wall was built around the island in just 110 days.

22
00:01:35,626 --> 00:01:40,379
The stones came from Bamseom, the island next to it. They blew it up with dynamite.

23
00:01:40,379 --> 00:01:46,428
In 1971, the runway was gone. It became a big square, and now Yeouido Park.

24
00:01:46,428 --> 00:01:52,333
Where the 333 m Parc1 stands, planes took off 50 years ago.

25
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:57,100
Why is Seokchon Lake cut in two by a road?

26
00:01:57,100 --> 00:02:00,900
This lake was once a part of the Han River, called the Songpa River.

27
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:05,200
Jamsil was an island between the Songpa and Sincheon rivers.

28
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:10,250
In 1971, they blocked the Songpa River with dirt and joined the island to land.

29
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:14,450
The part of the river that was left is now Seokchon Lake.

30
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:18,950
Songpa-daero road goes through the middle, so it became East Lake and West Lake.

31
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:24,600
Then, on rock 38 m underground, the 555 m Lotte World Tower went up.

32
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:30,000
The lake path we walk on was a river about 50 years ago.

33
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,250
Jamsugyo, the bridge you always see in rainy season news, is under water again.

34
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:38,800
But this bridge was made to go under water from the start.

35
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:43,650
It sits low, right above the Han River, so when the water rises, it goes under.

36
00:02:43,650 --> 00:02:48,000
A high bridge costs more and takes longer, so they built it low, short, and fast.

37
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,150
Then they put Banpo Bridge on top as a second floor.

38
00:02:51,150 --> 00:02:54,600
So it became a rare two-level bridge.

39
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:59,200
When water rises, cars are stopped first. When it rises more, people too.

40
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:04,050
When the water goes down, they wash off the mud, and cars drive again like nothing happened.

41
00:03:05,067 --> 00:03:09,667
Cheonggyecheon flows through the middle of Seoul. Where does its water come from?

42
00:03:09,667 --> 00:03:14,317
In fact, it used to be a stream that almost dried up when there was no rain.

43
00:03:14,317 --> 00:03:18,167
So most of the water flowing now is brought in by people.

44
00:03:18,167 --> 00:03:22,567
They pump water from the Han River, clean it, and send it through underground pipes.

45
00:03:22,567 --> 00:03:26,217
Groundwater that comes up in subway stations is added too.

46
00:03:26,217 --> 00:03:30,367
Over 100,000 tons of water a day pour out at the top of the stream.

47
00:03:30,367 --> 00:03:34,867
It flows over 5 km, through Jungnangcheon, and back into the Han River.

48
00:03:34,867 --> 00:03:39,367
So Cheonggyecheon is a path for Han River water to loop through Seoul.

49
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:45,000
On a night of heavy rain, a giant tunnel wakes up under Yangcheon-gu, Seoul.

50
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,700
This area floods often. Streets used to go under water whenever it rained.

51
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:54,550
So they dug a tunnel 10 m wide, 40 m underground.

52
00:03:54,550 --> 00:03:58,600
It's about 4.7 km long, and much wider than a subway tunnel.

53
00:03:58,600 --> 00:04:03,150
Just before rainwater overflows, it pours down a shaft into the tunnel.

54
00:04:03,150 --> 00:04:07,200
This tunnel can hold up to 320,000 tons of rainwater.

55
00:04:07,200 --> 00:04:11,400
When the rain stops, pumps slowly push the water out to the Han River.

56
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:16,600
While we sleep, a giant water bowl under the city keeps our town safe.

57
00:04:17,600 --> 00:04:22,000
Why is a 600-year-old wall next to DDP, which looks like a spaceship?

58
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:26,050
In the Joseon Dynasty, soldiers trained here.

59
00:04:26,050 --> 00:04:29,800
In 1925, a sports stadium was built here.

60
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:33,700
Later it became Dongdaemun Stadium, and games were played for over 80 years.

61
00:04:33,700 --> 00:04:38,300
When they took down the stadium and dug, the old Seoul city wall came out of the dirt.

62
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:43,250
There was also Igansumun, an arch gate that let water flow under the wall.

63
00:04:43,250 --> 00:04:48,700
Digging took 348 days. They brought back 142 m of the wall.

64
00:04:48,700 --> 00:04:53,000
That's why an old Joseon wall stands right next to a future-looking building.

65
00:04:54,033 --> 00:04:58,683
Off the coast of Jeollabuk-do, a straight road cuts across the sea.

66
00:04:58,683 --> 00:05:03,633
It's Saemangeum, 33.9 km long, the longest sea wall in the world.

67
00:05:03,633 --> 00:05:07,783
Work started in 1991 and took 19 years to finish.

68
00:05:07,783 --> 00:05:11,633
They dumped rocks and dirt into the sea and built the wall bit by bit.

69
00:05:11,633 --> 00:05:15,483
The hardest part was closing the last gap.

70
00:05:15,483 --> 00:05:19,383
Seawater rushed out through the narrow gap very fast.

71
00:05:19,383 --> 00:05:23,683
So they built huge gates in the wall to control the sea level.

72
00:05:23,683 --> 00:05:28,183
Inside the wall, new land and a lake appeared, about two-thirds the size of Seoul.

73
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:34,150
Have you heard that a robot comes out when the green dome of the National Assembly opens?

74
00:05:34,150 --> 00:05:36,950
Here's the answer first: the dome doesn't open.

75
00:05:36,950 --> 00:05:40,750
The dome is 64 m wide and weighs about 1,000 tons.

76
00:05:40,750 --> 00:05:44,350
It wasn't in the first plan. They added it while building.

77
00:05:44,350 --> 00:05:49,750
It was red copper at first, but it rusted over time and turned green.

78
00:05:49,750 --> 00:05:53,950
The 24 pillars around the building stand for the 24 seasons of the year.

79
00:05:53,950 --> 00:05:58,550
The dome means many opinions come together into one through debate.

80
00:05:58,550 --> 00:06:02,450
It doesn't open, but inside, the country's decisions are made every day.

81
00:06:03,033 --> 00:06:06,283
Your checked suitcase at the airport... how does it find your plane?

82
00:06:06,283 --> 00:06:10,383
Behind the check-in desk, your bag goes onto a belt under the floor.

83
00:06:10,383 --> 00:06:15,033
Under Incheon Airport Terminal 2, the belts are 96 km long.

84
00:06:15,033 --> 00:06:20,333
First, an X-ray machine checks the bag for dangerous things.

85
00:06:20,333 --> 00:06:24,783
Then your flight number is set, and the bag rushes off at 7 m per second.

86
00:06:24,783 --> 00:06:30,033
At every turn, the path changes by itself, so it reaches your plane.

87
00:06:30,033 --> 00:06:34,483
It moves 180,000 bags a day. Only 2 in a million miss the plane.

88
00:06:34,483 --> 00:06:39,633
While we walk to the gate, our bags race to the plane under our feet.
